.SH NAME
xara\-keygen \- make the table files for a named HF keying
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]xara\-keygen\f[R] \f[B]\-\-name\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
\f[B]\-\-out\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R]
.SH DESCRIPTION
\f[B]xara\-keygen\f[R] writes the table files for a named HF waveform
keying.
Stations only hear each other if they load the same keying, so a group
can share a channel and react only to its own members.
Load the result in \f[B]xara\f[R](1) or \f[B]xara\-monitor\f[R](1) with
\f[B]\-\-vara\-dir\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R].
.PP
The name is the whole definition: the same name gives the same files on
every machine.
A named keying is not encryption and gives no privacy.
It cannot talk to VARA or to stations on any other keying.
On amateur bands, publish the name you use.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\f[B]\-\-name\f[R] \f[I]NAME\f[R]
Name of the keying: 1\-64 characters of A\-Z, a\-z, 0\-9, \(aq\-\(aq,
\(aq_\(aq and \(aq.\(aq.
Case sensitive.
.TP
\f[B]\-\-out\f[R] \f[I]DIR\f[R]
Directory to write, created if needed.
It must not already hold table files.
.TP
\f[B]\-h\f[R], \f[B]\-\-help\f[R], \f[B]\-V\f[R], \f[B]\-\-version\f[R]
Print the usage or the version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
\f[I]DIR\f[R]/Binary1.dat, Binary2.dat, Binary3.dat, Mod1.dat, Mod2.dat
The tables.
.TP
\f[I]DIR\f[R]/xara\-keying.txt
Name, generator version and control\-code seed offset.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
\f[B]0\f[R]
Success.
.TP
\f[B]1\f[R]
The files could not be written.
.TP
\f[B]2\f[R]
Usage error.
.SH SEE ALSO
\f[B]xara\f[R](1), \f[B]xara\-monitor\f[R](1)
